      <td><code>write_build_report()</code> (unless <code>--no-report</code>)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr class="persist">
      <td><code>*.report.html</code></td>
      <td>same directory as the STL</td>
      <td>Permanent</td>
      <td><code>write_build_report()</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr class="persist">
      <td><code>build/scad_defaults.json</code></td>
      <td><code>build/</code></td>
      <td>Permanent (regenerated by <code>just defaults</code>)</td>
      <td><code>mastertray.py dump-defaults</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr class="temp">
      <td><code>%TEMP%\orca-pc-*\*.gcode</code></td>
      <td>system temp, random prefix</td>
      <td><strong>Auto-deleted</strong> — Python <code>TemporaryDirectory</code> context manager</td>
      <td><code>_slice_to_gcode()</code> in <code>check_printable.py</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr class="temp">
      <td><code>%TEMP%\mastertray-matrix-*\*.stl</code></td>
      <td>system temp, random prefix</td>
      <td><strong>Auto-deleted</strong> on success; kept on failure or <code>--keep</code></td>
      <td><code>build_matrix.py</code></td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     STAGE DESCRIPTIONS
════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<h2>Stage Details</h2>

<div class="stages">

<div class="stage">
  <h3>1 — User / Inputs</h3>
  <p>
    Three persistent inputs feed every build. <strong>mapping.yaml</strong> translates
    friendly names (<em>Container</em>, <em>Snap (Outer Wall)</em>) to OpenSCAD
    Customizer variable names (<code>Part_To_Build</code>, <code>LID_SNAP_EXT</code>).
    It is schema-validated on every load via <code>validate_mapping()</code>.
    <strong>configs/*.yaml</strong> carry printer-specific settings (nozzle size,
    layer height, filament type). <strong>scad_defaults.json</strong> is a cache of
    MasterBuilder.scad's own Customizer defaults, generated by
    <code>just defaults</code>; it lets <code>drop_redundant()</code> skip passing
    <code>-D</code> flags whose value already matches the default.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="stage">
  <h3>2 — mastertray.py</h3>
  <p>
    The build pipeline has four steps. <code>validate_mapping()</code> checks
    structural and cross-reference rules in mapping.yaml and exits loudly on any
    violation — before OpenSCAD is ever invoked.
    <code>build_overrides()</code> translates the CLI arguments into a dict of
    Customizer variable names and values.
    <code>drop_redundant()</code> removes any entry whose value already matches the
    Customizer default (keeping the <code>-D</code> list minimal).
    <code>run_build()</code> assembles the final <code>openscad.exe -D …
    MasterBuilder.scad -o output.stl</code> command and runs it as a subprocess.
    Unless <code>--no-report</code> is passed, <code>write_build_report()</code>
    emits <code>*.report.yaml</code> and <code>*.report.html</code> alongside the STL.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="stage">
  <h3>3 — OpenSCAD</h3>
  <p>
    OpenSCAD evaluates MasterBuilder.scad with the supplied <code>-D</code>
    overrides. The CGAL kernel computes the boolean mesh from the
    <code>compile_manifest()</code> → factory chain, exports the STL, and exits.
    Build time is approximately 1.6 s per part after the BOSL2 include-dedup fix
    (was ~23 s before — re-parsing BOSL2 through ~11 redundant includes).
    All sub-files are included transitively through MasterBuilder.scad; none
    re-include BOSL2 or MasterEngine directly.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="stage">
  <h3>4 — Manifold Gate</h3>
  <p>
    <code>validSTL.py</code> (or <code>check_manifold()</code> inside
    <code>mastertray.py --validate</code>) loads the STL with pymeshlab and
    queries <code>non_two_manifold_edges</code> and <code>boundary_edges</code>.
    Both must be zero. <strong>Component count alone does not detect non-manifold
    edges</strong> — this was the gap that allowed non-manifold jar coupons to
    reach the print bed. Run <code>just validate-stl "STL/Test Prints"</code>
    before every batch print.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="stage">
  <h3>5 — Slicer Gate</h3>
  <p>
    <code>check_printable.py --slicer</code> slices the STL via OrcaSlicer
    (headless subprocess, ~1 s per part) into a temporary directory.
    The gcode is parsed for three signals:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Empty layers</strong> (<code>_empty_layers()</code>) — scans
    <code>; layer_z =</code> comments for z-gaps &gt; 1.5× the median layer
    height. A gap means the geometry had zero cross-section at that height
    (CGAL seam edge from face-to-face union). <em>Hard fail.</em></li>
    <li><strong>First-layer coverage</strong> — sums G1 extrusion path length
    on the first layer, divides by the bounding-box area × linewidth. Low
    coverage (&lt;10%) means sparse bed adhesion. Skipped when
    <code>fl_coverage_pct = 0%</code> (circular geometry uses G2/G3 arc moves
    which the parser does not yet handle). <em>Hard fail when &gt; 0%.</em></li>
    <li><strong>Overhang length</strong> — total extrusion length tagged
    <code>; FEATURE: Overhang</code>. Reliable only on solid (mesh-off)
    geometry; mesh teardrops are also tagged Overhang. <em>Hint only.</em></li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The gcode file is held in a <code>TemporaryDirectory</code> context manager
    and is automatically deleted when analysis completes.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="stage">
  <h3>6 — Regression Gate</h3>
  <p>
    <code>build_matrix.py</code> runs every public intent (26 cases) through
    <code>mastertray.py</code> with <code>STRICT_KEYS=true</code> (typo'd KEY
    constants cause a loud exit). Each STL lands in a temporary directory.
    After building, <code>component_bboxes.py</code> reports the connected-
    component count (topology check) and pymeshlab reports
    <code>non_two_manifold_edges</code>. On success the temp directory is
    deleted; on failure or with <code>--keep</code> the STLs are retained for
    inspection. <code>just check-build</code> runs the gate;
    <code>just check-build-manifold</code> additionally fails on any
    non-manifold edge.
  </p>
</div>

</div>

